================================================================================
PYNODE CONFIGURATION REFERENCE
================================================================================

This file documents every option in config.json.

config.json is the SINGLE source of truth and is REQUIRED:
- Deployed exe: config.json must sit in the same folder as the exe.
- Running from source: config.json must be in the working directory (repo root).
- A missing file, invalid JSON, or a missing key is a fatal startup error.
- Unknown keys are ignored with a [CONFIG] WARNING (catches typos).
- There are no built-in defaults and no environment variable overrides.
- Config is read once at startup; restart the node after changes.

================================================================================
RPC SERVER (Pynode's own JSON-RPC interface)
================================================================================

[rpc.host]
  Type: string
  Shipped value: "127.0.0.1"
  Description: Interface Pynode's HTTP JSON-RPC server binds to.
  Security: Keep on 127.0.0.1 unless you understand the exposure -
            the RPC server has NO authentication.

[rpc.port]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 8888
  Description: Port for Pynode's HTTP JSON-RPC server.
  Usage: curl -X POST http://localhost:8888 -d '{"method": "getinfo"}'

================================================================================
NETWORK CONFIGURATION
================================================================================

[network.desired_outbound_peers]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 12
  Description: Target number of outbound peer connections
  Range: 1-50 recommended
  Note: More peers = better redundancy but higher bandwidth

[network.max_peers]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 20
  Description: Maximum total connections (inbound + outbound)
  Note: Must be >= desired_outbound_peers

[network.enable_inbound]
  Type: boolean
  Shipped value: true
  Description: Accept incoming peer connections
  Set to false if behind firewall or NAT without port forwarding

[network.listen_port]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 8338
  Description: Port for inbound P2P connections
  Note: Port 8333 is standard Bitcoin, but likely taken by Bitcoin Core
  Firewall: Open this port if enable_inbound = true

[network.listen_host]
  Type: string
  Shipped value: "0.0.0.0"
  Description: Interface to listen on for inbound connections
  Values:
    "0.0.0.0" = all interfaces (recommended)
    "127.0.0.1" = localhost only (no external connections)

[network.start_peers]
  Type: array of [host, port] arrays
  Shipped value: List of 11 known Bitcoin nodes (IPv4 and IPv6)
  Description: Initial peers to connect to on startup
  Format: [["ip_or_hostname", port], ...]
  Examples:
    ["127.0.0.1", 8333]           # Local Bitcoin Core
    ["seed.bitcoin.sipa.be", 8333] # DNS seed
    ["2001:bc8::1", 8333]         # IPv6 address

[network.peer_cache_file]
  Type: string
  Shipped value: "peer_cache.json"
  Description: File to cache known-good peers between restarts
  Auto-generated: Do not edit manually

[network.peer_cache_min_count]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 6
  Description: Minimum number of successful peers before caching

================================================================================
PROTOCOL SETTINGS
================================================================================

[protocol.magic_mainnet]
  Type: hex string
  Shipped value: "F9BEB4D9"
  Description: Bitcoin mainnet magic bytes (network identifier)
  Warning: Do NOT change unless pointing the node at another chain
           (see Alternative Chain example below)

[protocol.version]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 70016
  Description: Bitcoin P2P protocol version
  Note: Version 70016 supports SegWit and compact blocks

[protocol.user_agent]
  Type: string
  Shipped value: "/pynode:0.1.0/"
  Description: Identifier sent to peers during handshake
  Format: "/client_name:version/"

[protocol.genesis_header]
  Type: hex string (exactly 80 bytes / 160 hex chars)
  Shipped value: Bitcoin mainnet genesis header
          (hashes to 000000000019d6...0a8ce26f)
  Description: The chain's genesis block header. Single source of truth
              for chain identity - HeaderChain and ForkHandler both
              derive from it.
  Note: To run an ALTERNATIVE chain, override this together with
        protocol.magic_mainnet (the two values define a chain), and
        point the node at a fresh runtime dir / empty headers.dat.
  Warning: If headers.dat does not start with this genesis, the node
           fails fast at startup with a clear error (mismatch guard).
           Do NOT change for mainnet.

================================================================================
BLOCKCHAIN SETTINGS
================================================================================

[blockchain.download_blocks_from_height]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: -1
  Description: Start height for the block backfiller. The backfiller scans
               from this height up to the header tip and downloads every
               block that is missing from local storage (gap fill, not just
               tail append).
  Values:
    -1 = auto: start from the lowest block already in storage
         (waits for the first block if storage is empty)
    0+ = explicit start height
  Example: 840000 to cover the full Runes era (~100+ GB of blocks)

[blockchain.fork_handler_max_depth]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 100
  Description: Track last N blocks for reorganization detection
  Range: 10-1000
  Note: Higher = can detect deeper reorgs but uses more memory

[blockchain.enable_tx_validation]
  Type: boolean
  Shipped value: true
  Description: Validate transactions in received blocks/mempool messages

[blockchain.backfill_inflight_per_peer]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 16
  Description: Max blocks requested-but-undelivered per peer at any moment.
               Blocks are dispensed from a queue and topped up as deliveries
               arrive, so a serving peer is never flooded with a huge getdata
               burst. Mirrors Bitcoin Core's 16-blocks-in-transit IBD limit.

[blockchain.backfill_blocks_per_min_per_peer]
  Type: integer
  Shipped value: 600
  Description: Cap on blocks requested from one peer per minute. 600 blocks
               of ~1.7 MB (Runes era) is ~17 MB/min = ~2.3 Mbps sustained -
               a reasonable ask of a volunteer node. Lower it to be gentler.

================================================================================
EXAMPLE CONFIGURATIONS
================================================================================

--- Alternative Chain (different genesis) ---
Chain identity = genesis header + network magic. Override both, point at
a fresh runtime dir (or delete the old headers.dat), and add that chain's
seed peers. No code changes required.
{
  "protocol": {
    "magic_mainnet": "<that chain's 4-byte magic, hex>",
    "genesis_header": "<that chain's 80-byte genesis header, hex>"
  },
  "network": {
    "start_peers": [["seed.example.chain", 9333]]
  }
}
Note: a stale mainnet headers.dat with a new genesis is rejected at
startup (mismatch guard) - use a clean runtime dir.

--- Behind Firewall ---
{
  "network": {
    "enable_inbound": false,
    "desired_outbound_peers": 8
  }
}

--- High-Performance Node ---
{
  "network": {
    "desired_outbound_peers": 20,
    "max_peers": 40,
    "enable_inbound": true
  },
  "blockchain": {
    "fork_handler_max_depth": 200
  }
}

================================================================================
TROUBLESHOOTING
================================================================================

Issue: "[CONFIG] FATAL: config.json not found"
Solution: config.json must sit next to the exe (deployed) or in the
          working directory (source). It ships with the build.

Issue: "[CONFIG] FATAL: invalid JSON in config.json"
Solution: Validate JSON syntax (use jsonlint.com or VS Code).
          The node refuses to start on a malformed config.

Issue: "[CONFIG] FATAL: missing required key ..."
Solution: Every documented key must exist. Copy the missing key from
          this reference or from a fresh shipped config.json.

Issue: "[CONFIG] WARNING: unknown key ..."
Solution: Typo or leftover from an older version - the key is ignored.

Issue: Changes not taking effect
Solution: Restart the node - config is loaded once at startup.

================================================================================
SECURITY NOTES
================================================================================

1. The RPC server has NO authentication. Keep rpc.host on 127.0.0.1
   unless the port is protected by other means.

2. File permissions: Make config.json writable only by the node user.

3. Inbound connections: Only enable if you understand port forwarding.
   Malicious peers cannot steal funds but can waste bandwidth.

